<h3 class="text-center mb-4 text-normal">
  Delegate like a boss with GitHub <%= Copilot::COPILOT_SWE_AGENT %>
</h3>

<p class="mb-3">
  You’ve been granted access to <%= Copilot::COPILOT_SWE_AGENT %> (public preview) by an administrator of the <%= link_to @organization_login, user_url(@organization_login) %> organization.
</p>

<p class="mb-3">
  With Copilot coding agent, you can <b>let Copilot handle tasks in the background</b> — from updating documentation to improving test coverage — while you focus actively on the work that really needs your attention.
</p>

<p class="mb-3">
  Before you can get started, an organization administrator will need to <%= link_to "enable the agent in the repository you're working in", "https://gh.io/copilot-coding-agent-repository-opt-in" %> - or if you have personal repositories owned by your own account, you can <%= link_to "enable them yourself", "https://gh.io/copilot-coding-agent-repository-opt-in" %> from your user settings.
</p>

<p class="mb-3">
  Once a repository has been enabled, you can hand a task to Copilot by assigning an issue to Copilot, or asking Copilot to open a pull request from Copilot Chat. Copilot will:
</p>

<p>
  <ul class="ml-3">
    <li>Immediately open a draft pull request and outline its plan</li>
    <li>Write code, and validate its changes with your automated tests and linter</li>
    <li>Request your review once it's done</li>
  </ul>
</p>

<p class="mb-3">
  As Copilot works, you can keep an eye on it with a new agent logs view that is integrated into the pull request.
</p>

<p class="mb-3">
  To find out more about <%= Copilot::COPILOT_SWE_AGENT %>, head to the <%= link_to "GitHub Docs", "https://gh.io/copilot-coding-agent-docs" %>.
</p>

<% content_for :footer_text do %>
  <p>
    You are receiving this because you are the owner of the <%= link_to "@#{@user_login}", user_url(@user_login) %> account, and an administrator has granted you access to <%= Copilot::COPILOT_SWE_AGENT %>.
  </p>
<% end %>
